33:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"ca_file"}]," validates the remote. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"cert_file"}]," and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"key_file"}]," are what the remote\nsees when it demands a client certificate, so they are only needed against a\nlistener with ",["$","code",null,{"children":"client_ca_file"}]," set (setting one without the other is a startup\nerror). Setting ",["$","code",null,{"children":"use_native_roots: true"}]," instead of ",["$","code",null,{"children":"ca_file"}]," uses the\noperating system's trust store, which is what you want against a public CA and\nnot what you want against your own; when it is set, ",["$","code",null,{"children":"ca_file"}]," is ignored with\na warning. The scheme and the block go together: a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"grpcs://"}]," address with no\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"tls_config"}]," is rejected at startup, and so is a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"tls_config"}]," on a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"grpc://"}],"\naddress."]}]
